y and  compromise and that's what uh president  biden's leadership has brought us in  nato and dealing with the eu  germany's been a real challenge  for years they've cozied up to russia  former german chancellor working very  closely with vladimir putin on many  issues and so it's been difficult  germany believed that its security was  safe and they could just pursue their  economic well-being germany's  transitioning out of that mindset  step by step  you know the hard thing about  about the west if you believe in  democracies is every democracy has its  own internal processes canada the united  states  germany britain  and um  it's it's just the strength of the  people that these democratic systems uh  work from  it's very easy if you're  in russia to deceive yourself and  believe that you can take an issue like  this and break the alliance i don't  think that russia can do this but i do  think that there will be  other bumps in the road where  some relief is given for this issue or  another that helps cement the alliance  together so i wouldn't be concerned  about this i'd be looking down the road  and remember at this point in this  struggle  the military factor is the dominant  factor  if we don't succeed on the military  basis right now then the rest of it  is not going to help ukraine so we've  got to get the assistance in especially  to these territorial defense forces now  that are being called on to take main  battle position  uh up in in the fight in the east  so  um  accept some diplomacy some resilience  some flexibility in the application of  the sanctions it's going to work out  general petraeus  well i'm in a broad agreement with  canada's decision it was a very tough  one  they  had support from the us and germany uh  encouragement we have to  reckon with the realities of energy  provision how important it is  especially for countries like germany  that have  been so heavily dependent on russian gas  over the years  um i'd like to  build on this though a bit uh and really  focus on  an irony of what has taken place because  in trying to make  russia great again what putin has really  done is make nato great again uh there's  a degree of unity in nato now that  hasn't been seen since the end of the  cold war  that was even true in the days leading  up to the invasion i was at the munich  security conference again this year  uh and the unity there uh already and  the invasion didn't start for another  couple of days following the end of that  gathering  that unity is something i hadn't seen  since i was writing speeches for the  sakyar uh during the cold war as a young  major  this has really been quite extraordinary  uh putin essentially pushed  finland and sweden into the arms of nato  uh it will lead to nato expansion this  will make the baltic sea  and largely nato controlled uh sea  uh and so this has truly backfired in  innumerable ways uh for russia  that includes of course the various  sanctions that have been placed on the  russian economy its financial system  putin's inner circle  that includes  sanctions in the energy area  it includes export controls that are  going to make it very very difficult for  russia to  build or replace the systems the  enormous quantity of systems that it has  lost on the battlefield  uh the number of tanks is staggering  that they've lost and again if you can't  get microchips there's no way that  you're going to be able to  build new systems this is why after all  they're going to iran of all countries  to get replacements uh for their drones  that they've lost in great numbers as  well  so again this has been an extraordinary  development uh as general clark noted  the unprovoked invasion of a  sovereign democratic nation  um  we clearly have to be on the side of  ukraine uh in this particular situation  this is the first invasion of that type  since the end of the cold war uh the  rules based order does uh hang in the  balance  um and it is a moment where nato has  come together in an extraordinary  fashion and by and large uh again  uh lead guided uh by the actions from  washington uh in this particular  situation  that said there are going to be very  difficult calls uh i think the issue of  the generator return to uh for uh  nordstrom one uh clearly was one of  those  uh but again i think canada weighed all  of the uh the options the information  the pros and the cons  and ultimately made a decision that i  think was  uh in the best interest really of all  involved because again  if a major country like germany uh is  deprived its energy  uh the cohesion that i've just described  the unity uh that has been truly  extraordinary uh since uh the invasion  of ukraine uh is going to be a potential  casualty as well  uh thanks sean petraeus of course you  could tell that the the stakeholder  advisory council is uh not monolithic  and we have often very different views  on certain things  including this issue but i certainly  welcome that uh general hillary would  you like to comment on on the turbine  question and the sanctions yeah paul  just just a few words really and i'm i'm  a slight bit i think more harsh  on the canadian government decision and  i wish it had been slightly different or  in fact different as simple as that i  think maybe we we are going to see two  things from this  and i'm worried about both number one is  russia is going to see this as a sign of  encouragement  that there will be  uh decisions made that will benefit them  as as we go longer out and and the  second thing i worry about is that we're  now at six point at six months you know  we're in the middle of summer  inflation is rearing its ugly head in  the west people are coming out of the  pandemic and want to travel and the  focus is anything but ukraine  and this decision  to send the turbines back to germany  to send the turbines back sorry  would be seen in in einstein as maybe  the time when we have now  not going like this on sanctions more  and